\paragraph{Small-initialisation dynamics and weight decay.}
From small initialisation, linear networks learn eigenmodes one at a time, at a rate set by the mode's signal and after a delay that grows like $\log(1/\text{init})$ divided by that rate~\citep{saxe2014exact,simon2023stepwise}.
Near zero, gradient descent on a factorised model acts as power iteration, and with weight decay a mode grows only if its signal exceeds the decay~\citep{li2021greedy}; depth lengthens the escape time~\citep{jacot2021saddle}.
Weight decay on a factorised map soft-thresholds the singular values of the product~\citep{ziyin2022exact,kobayashi2024weight}.
The EMA target makes training a two-time-scale system, and the EMA scaling rule of \citet{busbridge2023ema} is a statement about its time scale.
For masking, moment identities for masked covariances~\citep{bisulco2025masked,dong2025mask} and the missing-data threshold of spectral partial least squares~\citep{gjolbye2026pls} give the statistics a mask leaves to predict, but they say nothing about collapse dynamics.

\paragraph{Races, thresholds and deadlines without a decaying predictor.}
In diagonal linear networks and two-layer networks trained from small initialisation, a coordinate or neuron activates once an integrated utility exceeds a threshold of order $\log(1/\text{init})$, and the losers carry their norms into the next round~\citep{pesme2023saddle,kunin2025alternating}; this is the closest published form of the race, but there is no weight decay, so no window closes and every direction with positive utility eventually activates.
\citet{kleinman2023critical} describe competition between pathways in deep linear networks with the same conserved quantity and show that early deficits are permanent; their competition has no weight decay and no predictor, and the deficit is imposed by the data, not by a decaying gain.
\citet{lyu2023dichotomy} show that weight decay shrinking a large initial factor switches the implicit bias at $t\approx\log(\text{scale})/\lambda$, a global analogue of our per-direction deadline \eqref{eq:lamrace}.
In supervised networks, weight decay and augmentation matter mostly early in training~\citep{golatkar2019time}; E8 (\cref{sec:e8}) tests the corresponding premise for JEPA collapse and finds it does not hold in our ViT.
For homogeneous networks of order above two the early phase lasts a power of $1/\text{init}$ rather than its logarithm~\citep{kumar2024early}, which is the scaling of our deep-encoder threshold \eqref{eq:deep}; their analysis has no weight decay, so it gives no deadline.
Multiplicative gradient noise makes collapsed directions attractive~\citep{chen2023stochastic}, which suggests that minibatch noise narrows the windows; in our linear model it lowers the count at $p_0=0.7$ but raises it at $p_0=1.5$, and cannot reseed a dead direction (B3, \cref{app:ext}).
The only weight-decay schedule ablation for self-supervised learning reports downstream accuracy (a DINO ramp against fixed values) and not rank~\citep{morningstar2024augmentations}.

\paragraph{Adaptive optimisers.}
Under sign descent a mode born from a small seed grows linearly and is learned in bounded time~\citep{sabanayagam2026optimizer}, and sign descent removes the dependence of the rate on the signal strength~\citep{kunstner2024heavy}; this is (i) of \cref{sec:adamw}, where the factor $a_k^2$ through which weight decay wins the race is normalised away. Neither has weight decay or a predictor.
The re-escape condition (ii) is the adaptive edge of stability of \citet{cohen2022adaptive}, reached here because the preconditioner decays while the direction is collapsed.
Adam is not rotation-invariant~\citep{xie2024adam}, so our reduction holds only for coordinate-aligned modes; under rotation we test signs only.

\paragraph{Measuring collapse.}
Dimensional collapse was characterised for contrastive learning by \citet{jing2022dimensional}; the effective rank of the embeddings is a label-free predictor of downstream performance~\citep{garrido2023rankme}.
In I-JEPA the effective rank rises early and then falls, and the best probe accuracy is not at the maximal rank~\citep{thilak2024lidar}.
We use the effective rank and the embedding spectrum to measure collapse throughout.

